Cause: Applying the LOWER() function to the name column in the WHERE clause prevents the regular index on name from being used. Fixes (choose one):

1) Create a functional/index-on-expression that matches the query:
   CREATE INDEX idx_users_name_lower ON users (LOWER(name));
   -- Then the original query can use this index as written:
   SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE LOWER(name) = 'alice' LIMIT 10;

2) Or avoid the function by comparing to the exact stored value (if case matches):
   SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE name = 'Alice' LIMIT 10;

3) Or store/search using a case-insensitive type/collation (e.g., citext in PostgreSQL) so the existing index is usable for case-insensitive lookups.

Any of the above (1 or 2 or 3) will allow the planner to use an index instead of a full scan. The most direct fix without changing data is option 1: create the functional index on LOWER(name).
